package.json is JSON, a structured text format. This complete example declares the start script:
```json
{
  "name": "launch-demo",
  "scripts": {
    "start": "node main.js"
  }
}
```
Double quotes surround JSON property names and text values. Curly braces hold objects, colons separate names from values, and commas separate properties. The nested scripts object maps a script name to its command. JSON does not permit comments or trailing commas.

The browser exercise asks you to represent a command sequence as JavaScript data. This separate example prints two commands but does not execute them:
```javascript
const commands = ['cd launch-demo', 'npm start'];
console.log(commands.join('\n'));
```
The output is two lines: cd launch-demo, then npm start. The brackets create an array; join combines its strings; `\n` is the newline character. console.log shows the text. It does not change the browser's folder or launch a terminal.

